From 61a033d31e275ece27e26e7f7745217c27e147c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: suiyan <suiyan@sunmmio.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 16:54:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] arch/dadao: SE mode 48-bit stack base (DG-006a)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The DADAO SE process used stack_base = 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (63-bit), but DADAO RB
effective addresses are 48-bit and rb1 (the ABI stack pointer) is masked to 48
bits on every access. argsInit set rb1 = stack_min (a 63-bit address), so the
CPU accessed the masked-low address (0xFFFFFFFF_xxxx) while the stack page was
mapped high (0x7FFFFFFF_FFFF_xxxx) — map never matched access, and the generic
stack-growth fault fixup could not recognise it, so any program writing the rb1
data stack page-faulted (0xffffffffdfd8).

Move the stack base inside the 48-bit space (0x00007FFFFFFFF000) so the mask is
a no-op and mapped == accessed. Exposed by DADAO-0628 DL-059a arr_sum (first
E2E to write the rb1 data stack; prior gem5-passing tests stayed in
registers/RegRAS). QEMU was unaffected (its trampoline sets rb1 in-range).

Verified: DADAO-0628 arr_sum E2E now exits 10 on gem5 (was fatal fault);
four-way differential AGREE=198/DIVERGE=0, lit E2E 10/10 dual-backend.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/arch/dadao/process.cc | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/arch/dadao/process.cc b/src/arch/dadao/process.cc
index e0cdacb00b..715c89f3b6 100644
--- a/src/arch/dadao/process.cc
+++ b/src/arch/dadao/process.cc
@@ -23,7 +23,12 @@ Process::Process(const ProcessParams &params, loader::ObjectFile *objFile)
 {
     fatal_if(params.useArchPT, "Arch page tables not implemented for DADAO.");
 
-    const Addr stack_base = 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFL;
+    // DADAO RB effective addresses are 48-bit; the stack pointer (rb1) is
+    // masked to 48 bits on every memory access. A 63-bit stack base would be
+    // mapped high but accessed masked-low, so the mapped page never matches
+    // the accessed page and the stack-growth fault fixup can't recognise it.
+    // Keep the base inside the 48-bit space so mask is a no-op (map == access).
+    const Addr stack_base = 0x00007FFFFFFFF000L;
     const Addr max_stack_size = params.maxStackSize;
     const Addr next_thread_stack_base = stack_base - max_stack_size;
     const Addr brk_point = roundUp(image.maxAddr(), PageBytes);
-- 
